import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { ComboboxHero } from "../../demos/combobox/hero.rx"
import { ComboboxMultiple } from "../../demos/combobox/multiple.rx"
import { ComboboxGrouped } from "../../demos/combobox/grouped.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Combobox" subtitle="An input that filters a list of options to choose from.">
    <Demo source="combobox/hero"><ComboboxHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="combobox/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The popup is an element with the native <code>popover</code> attribute, placed against the input with CSS
      anchor positioning. The browser provides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The top layer: the popup is drawn above everything else, without a portal or a <code>z-index</code>.</li>
      <li>
        Placement below the input, flipped above it when it doesn't fit, and at least as wide as the input
        (<code>min-width: anchor-size(width)</code>).
      </li>
      <li>The text input itself: typing, selection, the caret, and a <code>placeholder</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The popup is <code>popover="manual"</code>, not <code>"auto"</code>: the browser's light dismiss counts a
      press on the input that opened the popup as a press outside, in Chrome, Firefox and Safari alike, so it would
      close the list as soon as you click into the input. The library closes it instead, on <kbd>Escape</kbd>, on
      {" "}<kbd>Tab</kbd> and on a press outside the input, its buttons and the popup.
    </p>
    <p>
      The library adds the rest: filtering the options as you type, the highlighted option (named by the input's
      {" "}<code>aria-activedescendant</code> while focus stays in the input), the arrow keys and <kbd>Enter</kbd>,
      the selected value and the form value it submits, chips for multiple selection, the ARIA roles and
      relationships, and <code>data-side</code> with the arrow's position, which the browser doesn't report.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The popup stays in the document while it is closed, so there is no Portal part, and the popup is the
        positioned element, so there is no Positioner or Backdrop. There is no Label part: put the combobox in a
        {" "}<code>FieldRoot</code> with a <code>FieldLabel</code>, as the demo above does, or give the input an
        {" "}<code>aria-label</code>. There is no InputGroup or Icon either: wrap the input and its buttons in an
        element of your own, and put an icon inside <code>ComboboxTrigger</code>. Options are written as markup, one
        {" "}<code>ComboboxItem</code> each, so there are no Collection or Row parts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="multiple">Multiple selection</h3>
    <p>
      With <code>multiple</code> the root holds <code>values</code> (with <code>defaultValues</code> and
      {" "}<code>onValuesChange</code>). Choosing an option toggles it, empties the input and leaves the list open.
      Put the input in <code>ComboboxChips</code> and the popup is anchored to that container instead, so it is as
      wide as the whole field. Render a <code>ComboboxChip</code> per selected value from the{" "}
      <code>combobox</code> context; <kbd>Backspace</kbd> in an empty input removes the last one. With a{" "}
      <code>name</code>, the form gets one entry per value.
    </p>
    <Demo source="combobox/multiple"><ComboboxMultiple /></Demo>

    <h3 id="grouped">Groups</h3>
    <p>
      <code>ComboboxGroup</code> and <code>ComboboxGroupLabel</code> put a heading over related options. A group
      whose options all fail the filter is hidden. A separator next to it isn't, so the demo's CSS hides it with
      {" "}<code>:has()</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo source="combobox/grouped"><ComboboxGrouped /></Demo>

    <h3 id="filtering">Filtering</h3>
    <p>
      An option matches when its label contains what was typed, ignoring case and accents: "creme" finds "Crème
      brûlée". The label is the option's <code>label</code> prop, else its text. Pass <code>filter</code> to
      match another way; it gets the label and the trimmed query. An option that doesn't match isn't rendered.
      With <code>autoHighlight</code> the first match is highlighted as you type, so <kbd>Enter</kbd> chooses it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>ComboboxEmpty</code> shows its children while no option matches, and <code>ComboboxStatus</code> holds
      any other message about the list. Both are <code>role="status"</code> elements that stay in the document;
      style the empty one with <code>:not(:empty)</code> so it takes no room while there are matches. The popup
      and the list carry <code>data-empty</code> then too.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> hold the selection; <code>null</code> is no selection.
      {" "}<code>inputValue</code> and <code>onInputValueChange</code> hold the text, and <code>open</code> and
      {" "}<code>onOpenChange</code> the popup. By default the input shows the selected option's label. Closing the
      list without choosing puts that label back, and emptying the input clears the selection.
    </p>
    <p>
      The input and <code>ComboboxValue</code> name a value by the label its option registered. A value whose
      option hasn't rendered (a controlled value set before the list exists) has no label yet; pass{" "}
      <code>items</code>, a list of <code>{"{ value, label }"}</code>, to name it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">Forms</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a <code>FieldRoot</code> that has one, the selected value is submitted
      with the form. Inside a field the input is labelled and described by it, and <code>required</code> is
      validated with the field's other constraints.
    </p>

    <h3 id="placement">Placement and the arrow</h3>
    <p>
      <code>side</code>, <code>align</code>, <code>sideOffset</code> and <code>alignOffset</code> on the popup
      become CSS anchor positioning declarations in its inline style, so a stylesheet can replace any of them.
      When the browser flips the popup, its <code>data-side</code> changes to the side it ended up on.{" "}
      <code>ComboboxArrow</code> is placed on the popup's edge that faces the input and carries the same{" "}
      <code>data-side</code>; give it a shape and turn it per side, as the Popover page shows.
    </p>

    <h3 id="animation">Animating it</h3>
    <p>
      Animate the popup in with <code>@starting-style</code> under <code>:popover-open</code>, and turn the
      animation's origin with <code>data-side</code>, as the first demo does. Closing hides a popover at once; to
      animate it out, transition <code>display</code> and <code>overlay</code> with <code>allow-discrete</code>,
      which takes effect in the engines that support it and is ignored in the others.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="combobox" />
  </DocsPage>
}
